---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// T120 — /product/coaching. Fourth per-pillar product page after
// desktop, mobile, and bridge. The coaching library is the doctrinal
// moat (ADR-013) and the engine that turns a WeaknessEpisode
// (ADR-005 §5.2) into an ActionPrescription (§5.3) with ActionOutcome
// (§5.4) closing the loop. Sub-task T121 (sample weaknesses +
// prescriptions) layers concrete examples onto this base.
//
// Doctrine lock: ADR-004 forbids LLM-driven coaching in the 6-month
// plan. Coaching is the single highest-risk pillar for a reader to
// silently infer "AI coach", so the page must explicitly frame the
// mechanism as rules-based — same defensive negation pattern as
// /product/desktop's "live coaching" section.

const TITLE = 'The coaching library — rules, prescriptions, outcomes — LAP';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'The coaching library is a catalog of weakness shapes — rules indexed by track section, car class, and weakness shape. Each rule is auditable YAML, versioned, and produces a prescription with a measurable success condition.';
---

<BaseLayout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION}>
  <main class="px-grid-margin mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl py-24 sm:py-32">
    <header class="max-w-prose">
      <p class="text-acid-green text-caption tracking-wide uppercase">Coaching library</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-h1 text-chalk tracking-display mt-4 text-balance">
        The coaching library.
      </h1>
      <p class="text-steel text-body mt-6">
        Every prescription in LAP comes from a rule in the coaching library. The library is what
        decides which patterns count as a weakness, which drill to put in front of you, and how to
        tell whether it worked. It is the surface where doctrine and code meet.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-12">
      <section
        aria-labelledby="coaching-library-heading"
        class="border-steel/15 max-w-prose border-t pt-10"
      >
        <p class="text-steel text-caption tracking-wide uppercase">The library</p>
        <h2
          id="coaching-library-heading"
          class="font-display text-h3 text-chalk tracking-display mt-3 text-balance"
        >
          A catalog of weakness shapes.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          The library is a versioned set of rules — small, named, written down. Each rule is a
          weakness pattern with an explanation, a prescription, and a measurable success condition.
          They are indexed by three axes: the
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">track section</strong>
          the pattern shows up in, the
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">car class</strong>
          it applies to, and the
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">weakness shape</strong>
          itself — late-on-the-brakes, off-throttle-too-early, oversteer-on-exit. The rule that fires
          on you depends on which combination matched your session, not on a one-size-fits-all tip sheet.
        </p>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          The library is rule-based, not a chat box. A weakness has to match a written-down
          detection condition before it surfaces, and a prescription has to cite the rule it came
          from. That framing matters because the alternative — a model that talks to you — is
          something we have ruled out for the first phase of LAP for a reason: an opaque coach you
          cannot audit is not a coach you can trust.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        aria-labelledby="coaching-rule-to-prescription-heading"
        class="border-steel/15 max-w-prose border-t pt-10"
      >
        <p class="text-steel text-caption tracking-wide uppercase">Rule → prescription</p>
        <h2
          id="coaching-rule-to-prescription-heading"
          class="font-display text-h3 text-chalk tracking-display mt-3 text-balance"
        >
          From a detected weakness to a drill you can do tomorrow.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          When a session lands on the platform, the detector walks the library and records every
          rule whose condition matched. Each match becomes a
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">weakness episode</strong> — when it happened, where on
          the lap, how severe, how confident. The rule then produces a
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">prescription</strong>: a small piece of work with a
          plain-language rationale, a drill to do next session, and a success condition the platform
          will check against your future laps.
        </p>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          A prescription is not a vibe. It has a target metric, a target value, and a measurement
          window — three more sessions, seven more days, the next race weekend. You can see exactly
          which rule fired it, what evidence triggered the rule, and what would count as the
          prescription having worked.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        aria-labelledby="coaching-outcomes-heading"
        class="border-steel/15 max-w-prose border-t pt-10"
      >
        <p class="text-steel text-caption tracking-wide uppercase">Outcomes</p>
        <h2
          id="coaching-outcomes-heading"
          class="font-display text-h3 text-chalk tracking-display mt-3 text-balance"
        >
          And then we measure whether it worked.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          When the measurement window closes, the platform records an
          <strong class="text-chalk font-medium">outcome</strong>: did the success condition hold,
          how much did the metric move, what fraction of the gap to your reference closed. Outcomes
          are how a prescription stops being a suggestion and starts being a fact. Either it worked
          for you or it did not, on numbers you can read.
        </p>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          Outcomes also feed back into the library itself. Rules whose prescriptions consistently
          fail to move the needle for drivers in a given car-and-track context get retired or
          re-tuned; rules whose prescriptions consistently work get reinforced. The library improves
          on the back of measurement, not on the back of a model retrain.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        aria-labelledby="coaching-auditable-heading"
        class="border-steel/15 max-w-prose border-t pt-10"
      >
        <p class="text-steel text-caption tracking-wide uppercase">Auditable, not opaque</p>
        <h2
          id="coaching-auditable-heading"
          class="font-display text-h3 text-chalk tracking-display mt-3 text-balance"
        >
          Rules you can read.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          Each rule is a small, named, versioned object — readable by us, readable by you when the
          platform shows you why a prescription fired. That is the deliberate trade. We are giving
          up the surface area of an open-ended chat in exchange for a coaching surface where every
          recommendation has a clear chain of custody: this rule, on this evidence, with this
          expected gain, measured against this success condition.
        </p>
        <p class="text-steel text-body leading-body mt-4">
          The pieces that make a session-by-session coach work — pattern matching, prescription,
          outcome measurement — are not the pieces that need a model that talks. They need a library
          that is written down. That is the bet of the first phase of LAP, and the coaching library
          is the artefact that proves it.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-16">
      <a
        href="/product"
        class="text-chalk hover:text-acid-green text-body inline-flex items-center underline underline-offset-4 transition"
      >
        ← Back to product overview
      </a>
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
